  "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/b0b7160eef056eccd922fbd6264cb247b9f787bb431c51ff3345be39aa26b872.png)\n\nNote that in $\u25b3OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=1.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=1.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+1.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+1.5b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1.5\n$$",
